norm pruning is currently running with k=0.9 and 0.95, alongside seed43 random pruning at k=0.5 and 0.7, while RoboFlamingo's Perceiver Resampler connector is still in the planning phase. I also need to track the X-VLA seed44 pruning evaluations at k=0.7 and k=0.9.\n\nFor the worst-case scenario, I'm defining it as: OpenVLA VTC-only completely failing with 0% success rate, the L2-norm pruning diverging significantly from random pruning by more than 15 percentage points which would expose a confound in the pruning method, and X-VLA k=0.7 seed44 dropping well below 35% success rate.\n\nLooking at the expected metric ranges, I'm seeing OpenVLA VTC-only potentially hitting 0-5% for both m32 and m64, L2-norm k=0.9 around 40-52%, k=0.95 around 50-60%, X-VLA k=0.7 seed44 between 20-35%, and SmolVLA VTR m32 between 58-65%. The narrative is shifting away from a clean four-class taxonomy toward a more cautionary framing that highlights two stark extremes: the catastrophic collapse from pixel-shuffling failures on one end.\n\nNow I'm mapping out the specific experiments that need predictions, starting with the L2-norm pruning evaluations currently running. Looking at the remaining experiments from the smart compression evaluations, I've got results across different model sizes and seeds—most showing completion with varying success rates. I'm also tracking the VTC-only training runs that need evaluation once they finish, plus the planned roboflamingo baseline and pruning work. I've got nine experiments actively running across the openvla and xvla pruning evaluations, plus one more planned for the roboflamingo connector. Let me scan through the unified pruning protocol to see what else should be queued up that hasn't started yet.\n\nFor the d108 protocol, I need to test L2 norm pruning on OpenVLA across k values of 0.3, 0.5, 0.7, 0.9, and 0.95—currently only 0.9 and 0.95 are running, so the others are still queued. Now I'll structure out predictions for each experiment plan and lay out the full YAML with worst-case scenarios for each one.\n\nThe worst case for d108 is if L2 norm pruning significantly outperforms random pruning on OpenVLA (>15 percentage points difference), which would mean the MLP projector doesn't distribute importance uniformly and the comparison between OpenVLA's random pruning and X-VLA's L2 norm pruning becomes unfair—the taxonomy separation we're seeing could be partly due to the pruning method difference rather than pure connector differences. At k=0.9, random pruning gave 62%, so if L2 norm is substantially better, it should score higher, not lower—I had that backwards initially. The expected range for OpenVLA L2 norm at k=0.9 is [40, 52], which actually suggests L2 norm performs worse, not better, so that would be the best-case scenario for our comparison validity. I'm realizing there's a potential confound here—if X-VLA was only tested with L2 norm pruning while OpenVLA used random removal, the comparison might be conflating pruning method with architecture. But looking back at the framing, this actually strengthens the findings: OpenVLA's robustness to random pruning is the harder test, while X-VLA's cliff even with importance-based pruning is the easier test. The real issue is that L2 norm appears actively harmful for OpenVLA (15+ percentage points worse than random), which contradicts the idea that importance-based pruning should be \"easier.\" using because typically importance-based pruning should be ≥ random. If L2 norm is worse than random for OpenVLA, it suggests L2 norm is a bad importance metric for MLP projectors, which means the cross-model comparison using L2 norm offline diagnostic may not be valid.\n\nActually, the range says [40, 52] for k=0.9 and [50, 60] for k=0.95. The existing random results are 62% and 69.4%. So L2 norm is 10-22pp worse than random. This is quite damaging because importance-based pruning performs worse than random for OpenVLA, while X-VLA was tested with L2 norm and showed the cliff. If X-VLA were tested with random pruning instead, the cliff might shift or vanish entirely, which means the taxonomy comparison between OpenVLA-random and X-VLA-L2norm isn't actually apples-to-apples. The extremes still hold up though—pixel-shuffle remains catastrophic and pure linear stays immune regardless of method.\n\nFor X-VLA with seed44, the k=0.7 results drop dramatically to [20, 35]% compared to the original 56.2%, suggesting the cliff is seed-dependent and not as robust as I thought. And OpenVLA's VTC-only variant fails across the board.\n\nI'm also tracking SmolVLA VTR seeds which are mostly complete, and I need to test OpenVLA random with different seeds to check reproducibility of the pruning curve. Let me organize all the experiments systematically and write this up properly.\n\nI'm going through the experiment plan and registry to identify what's still in progress or running. From the plan, I have five experiments at various stages: the SmolVLA reencoding work, OpenVLA method universality tests, the M-sweep, the unified pruning protocol, and the RoboFlamingo connector which probably won't finish in time. The registry shows four experiments currently running across different OpenVLA configurations and pruning evaluation.\n\nLooking at the full list of running evaluations, I see there are more pruning experiments than what's covered in the plan—various L2 norm and random pruning variants at different sparsity levels, plus some XVLA pruning runs. The d108 protocol mentions L2 norm pruning at k=0.3, 0.5, and 0.7 for OpenVLA, but only the higher sparsity levels (0.9 and 0.95) are currently running, so I need to account for the missing ones. The RoboFlamingo work from c5 hasn't started yet. Now I'm putting together a comprehensive YAML that captures all the planned and in-progress experiments with their full details.\n\nFor the L2 norm pruning results, I'm estimating performance based on how the pruning strategy interacts with the model architecture. L2 norm pruning on the MLP projectors performs worse than random pruning because the L2 norm doesn't actually correlate with task relevance in these non-linear layers—tokens with low magnitude can still carry important spatial or action information after the MLP's mixing, so L2 norm systematically removes tokens that turn out to be critical despite their low norm values. Random pruning, by contrast, maintains a more balanced distribution of information across the remaining tokens.\n\nFor the xvla model with different random seeds, the seed variation at k=0.7 shows a dramatic performance drop, suggesting this is a critical pruning threshold where the model becomes sensitive to which specific tokens are retained. the same ballpark even with seed43's random pruning variance, probably landing around 43-55%. For k=0.5, I'd expect similar degradation patterns, dropping to maybe 31-43%. The training runs with different M values all hit the worst tier—completing but producing near-zero success rates due to overfitting. The RoboFlamingo connector is planned but unlikely to finish in time.\n\nNow I'm going back through the plan to match experiment IDs precisely. Looking at the c4_learned_reencoding_smolvla group, most work is done—the remaining three training runs are in analysis phase with their evals already complete, showing success rates around 54-58%. This group is essentially finished.\n\nFor c4_ext_method_universality_openvla, I need to focus on the VTC-only experiments at M=32 and M=64 since the M=128 results are already in. The c4_ext_openvla_msweep group covers OpenVLA VTC training across M=32, 64, and 96. Then there's d108_unified_pruning_protocol with L2 norm pruning on OpenVLA plus the seed43 random pruning and X-VLA seed44 variants. The c5_third_connector_roboflamingo group hasn't started yet.\n\nNow I'm structuring the YAML output by plan_id with individual experiments nested underneath, making sure to capture all the planned work comprehensively.\n\nFor d108_unified_pruning_protocol, the L2 norm pruning sweeps across k values (0.3, 0.5, 0.7, 0.9, 0.95) are meant to unify the pruning methodology with X-VLA. The registry shows k=0.9 and k=0.95 are currently running despite the plan description saying they're queued, so the status has progressed since the plan was written. There's also a sweep variant in PREPARING state. For the X-VLA seed experiments, I need to check what's queued or running there as well.\n\nWriting YAML config... Adding experiment details... Generating comparison table...\n\nThe L2 norm pruning method significantly underperforms random pruning by about 16 percentage points at k=0.9, which suggests that a substantial portion of the performance gap between OpenVLA-random and X-VLA-L2norm might stem from the pruning method itself rather than connector architecture differences. This complicates the clean separation claimed between MLP-graded and threshold-graded patterns, though interestingly, even when using the same L2 norm method, OpenVLA still exhibits a graded degradation pattern while X-VLA shows a cliff pattern—the fundamental difference persists, just potentially amplified by the method confound.\n\nNow I'm looking at the expected results for OpenVLA with L2-norm pruning at k=0.95, where the performance gap versus random pruning remains consistent at around 14 percentage points, suggesting this method disadvantage is stable across different pruning thresholds. 在k=0.5的更激进pruning下，L2 norm方法仍然表现不佳，虽然两种方法都严重退化，但L2 norm持续劣于随机baseline约15个百分点，说明这个confound问题在不同pruning强度下都很顽固。\n\n当进一步降低到k=0.3时，两种方法的性能都接近零，gap自然收窄到约13个百分点，但方向保持一致，确认L2 norm的劣势不是噪声而是系统性的。\n\n现在我在检查X-VLA在seed44下的可重复性，特别是在k=0.7这个最陡峭的降级区间，预期看到与seed42类似的cliff pattern。\n\n关键是seed44可能采样到了对剪枝更敏感的任务分布，比如\"pick_up_the_alphabet_soup\"这类依赖精确空间推理的任务，导致cliff点左移。L2范数排序在不同batch中的微小变化，加上batch normalization的随机性，可能让seed44的token移除恰好击中BART编码器的关键跨token依赖，使得28%的结果远低于预期的-1σ范围。\n\n现在看到的对比数据表明，cliff的位置和陡峭程度对seed高度敏感，无法作为模型架构的稳健特征。\n\n28%对比56.2%的差异远超正常seed变异范围，说明X-VLA